md("""**Bottom line:** breakdowns cost this fleet well over a million AZN a year, mostly in lost haulage. Two cheap fixes come first: **stop PMs going late** and **control overloading on South Pit**. On top of that, the trucks' own sensors give a warning of several days for most failures. A **per-truck z-score rule with a cost-tuned threshold** pays for its inspections many times over. The gradient-boosted model ranks risk better (higher PR-AUC) and, at a similar inspection load, catches about 10 points more failures, but it earns only ~2% more, because its extra catches are mostly cheap hydraulic and suspension faults. An honest result: **go live with the simple rule first**, then add the model where truck availability is worth more than inspection hours.

*Caveats: synthetic data with a known structure, so real precursors will be noisier. Money figures rest on the stated assumptions (420 AZN/h margin, 80% find rate, 35%/30% planned cost/downtime). An inspection can also find faults other than the predicted one, which this evaluation ignores (conservative). The model should be retrained quarterly and monitored for drift as trucks age.*""")

